1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,407
A reviewer opens a pull request
that had five commits yesterday.

2
00:00:08,447 --> 00:00:13,156
A commit is one saved snapshot of the
project, and a pull request is GitHub's

3
00:00:13,196 --> 00:00:16,795
proposal to merge a branch of
commits. Today it lists nine.

4
00:00:16,835 --> 00:00:21,730
Three subjects appear twice, and a merge
commit that nobody intended joins them.

5
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:25,184
A merge commit is a commit
that joins two lines of work.

6
00:00:25,224 --> 00:00:30,303
A second reviewer opens the same pull
request, looks at the changed files, and sees

7
00:00:30,343 --> 00:00:33,140
nothing wrong: the same
three files as before.

8
00:00:33,480 --> 00:00:37,341
Both reviewers are describing the
same pull request accurately.

9
00:00:37,381 --> 00:00:42,543
How can both be right? Hold that question.
Somebody rebased, that is, copied commits

10
00:00:42,583 --> 00:00:47,621
onto a new base. Somebody pulled, somebody
pushed, and no command printed an error

11
00:00:47,661 --> 00:00:52,513
at any point. Now a branch that two people
share has to be rebuilt, in front of

12
00:00:52,553 --> 00:00:55,051
reviewers, without losing anybody's work.

13
00:00:55,391 --> 00:01:00,726
A first question, before the cause is named.
Is this a display problem on the page,

14
00:01:00,766 --> 00:01:05,712
or the real content of the repository?
Which read-only checks would tell you?

15
00:01:05,752 --> 00:01:06,878
Say them out loud.

16
00:01:11,718 --> 00:01:16,434
Ask the repository and not the page: git
ls-remote for what the server holds, and a

17
00:01:16,474 --> 00:01:21,191
local log of the branch. If they list nine
commits too, the page isn't the problem.

18
00:01:23,031 --> 00:01:27,075
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

19
00:01:27,115 --> 00:01:31,627
This is a debrief of Incident 5,
incidents/05-rebased-shared-branch.

20
00:01:31,667 --> 00:01:35,912
You must have generated it and
attempted it, with a command log.

21
00:01:35,952 --> 00:01:38,925
Have you? If you haven't,
stop the video now.

22
00:01:41,265 --> 00:01:43,298
The cause is named in the next section.

23
00:01:43,638 --> 00:01:49,005
This incident is also the first of three
scenarios that define what the course calls

24
00:01:49,045 --> 00:01:52,673
the senior standard. The
standard isn't a harder command.

25
00:01:52,713 --> 00:01:58,080
It's a complete response in eleven steps,
carried out in order, under time pressure,

26
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:03,357
with other people's clones involved. In
video 59 you learned what a rebased shared

27
00:02:03,397 --> 00:02:08,570
branch does to its users, and in video 125
why a pull request can show unexpected

28
00:02:08,610 --> 00:02:13,912
commits. Today you do the whole response:
from inspecting the state to preventing a

29
00:02:13,952 --> 00:02:14,363
repeat.

30
00:02:14,703 --> 00:02:18,700
Watch for one property above all:
steps 1 to 6 change nothing.

31
00:02:20,540 --> 00:02:21,952
After this video you can:

32
00:02:22,292 --> 00:02:25,705
Recognize a pull request in which
every commit appears twice.

33
00:02:26,045 --> 00:02:29,604
Reconstruct from refs and reflogs
who rewrote what and when.

34
00:02:29,944 --> 00:02:32,789
Preserve every clone's
state before rebuilding.

35
00:02:33,129 --> 00:02:37,827
Rebuild the branch so that each change is
present once and nobody's work is lost.

36
00:02:38,167 --> 00:02:42,271
Carry out the eleven steps of the senior
standard, from inspecting state to

37
00:02:42,311 --> 00:02:43,486
preventing recurrence.

38
00:02:45,326 --> 00:02:49,678
What happened, as a mechanism. Commits
that another person had built on were

39
00:02:49,718 --> 00:02:54,358
rewritten, and that person's git pull,
configured to merge, joined both versions.

40
00:02:54,398 --> 00:02:58,229
A rewritten shared branch met a
clone that still had the originals.

41
00:02:58,269 --> 00:03:03,025
The merge made both series reachable from
one tip, the newest commit of the branch,

42
00:03:03,065 --> 00:03:04,528
and the push published it.

43
00:03:04,868 --> 00:03:10,148
Why the two reviewers disagree. The commit
list of a pull request is main..head, the

44
00:03:10,188 --> 00:03:13,758
commits the head branch has
and main lacks: nine entries.

45
00:03:13,798 --> 00:03:18,888
The file view is the diff main...head,
what changed since the two split: the same

46
00:03:18,928 --> 00:03:24,018
three files as before, because two copies
of one change are one change in a tree.

47
00:03:24,058 --> 00:03:27,881
The commit list and the file
view answer different questions.

48
00:03:27,921 --> 00:03:29,718
So both reviewers were right.

49
00:03:30,058 --> 00:03:33,522
What is not at fault.
--force-with-lease isn't at fault.

50
00:03:33,562 --> 00:03:38,466
It's a forced push that succeeds only if
the server's branch is still where you

51
00:03:38,506 --> 00:03:43,472
expect it, so it protects the server's
commits, not the unpublished commits in a

52
00:03:43,512 --> 00:03:48,728
teammate's clone. And the rebase itself lost
nothing and changed nothing in content.

53
00:03:48,768 --> 00:03:52,795
The damage is entirely the merge
that made both series reachable.

54
00:03:52,835 --> 00:03:53,483
Layer: Git.

55
00:03:53,823 --> 00:03:55,925
The senior standard: eleven steps.

56
00:03:56,265 --> 00:03:59,892
Inspect the state. Inspect
refs. Inspect the reflog.

57
00:03:59,932 --> 00:04:03,770
Identify the old branch state.
Understand what changed.

58
00:04:03,810 --> 00:04:08,212
Preserve recoverable references.
Determine the safest recovery.

59
00:04:08,252 --> 00:04:12,443
Restore the correct history.
Update the pull request safely.

60
00:04:12,483 --> 00:04:15,405
Explain what happened. Prevent recurrence.

61
00:04:15,745 --> 00:04:19,117
Steps 1 to 5 are the read-only
phase of the method.

62
00:04:19,157 --> 00:04:24,736
Step 6 is the preserve phase. Steps 7 to 9
are the change phase, and steps 10 and 11

63
00:04:24,776 --> 00:04:26,609
are the part that isn't Git.

64
00:04:26,949 --> 00:04:28,445
The options at step 7.

65
00:04:28,785 --> 00:04:33,017
The lease at step 9. The restoring
push is itself a forced push.

66
00:04:33,057 --> 00:04:38,024
It names the value you examined:
--force-with-lease=<branch>:<expected-id>.

67
00:04:38,064 --> 00:04:43,231
If a teammate pushed after your fetch,
the push is refused and you look again.

68
00:04:43,271 --> 00:04:48,705
A bare --force would overwrite their work.
Tell the teammates before the push, not

69
00:04:48,745 --> 00:04:49,106
after.

70
00:04:49,446 --> 00:04:54,624
GitHub, not Git. After a forced push to a
head branch, review comments attached to

71
00:04:54,664 --> 00:04:59,715
replaced commits are shown as outdated,
and an approval is dismissed if the rule

72
00:04:59,755 --> 00:05:03,915
"dismiss stale pull request approvals"
is on and the diff changed.

73
00:05:03,955 --> 00:05:07,988
Here the diff is identical, and
the commit list is what changed.

74
00:05:08,028 --> 00:05:13,079
Confirm on GitHub with gh pr view <number>
--json commits,changedFiles and gh pr

75
00:05:13,119 --> 00:05:17,342
diff <number> --name-only. Those
commands are shown without output.

76
00:05:17,382 --> 00:05:20,461
Nothing was captured from
GitHub for this course.

77
00:05:20,801 --> 00:05:25,828
Prevention. git config set pull.rebase
true: when an upstream, the branch you pull

78
00:05:25,868 --> 00:05:31,018
from, was rewritten, git pull --rebase finds
the old fork point in the reflog of the

79
00:05:31,058 --> 00:05:34,293
remote-tracking branch and
replays only your commits.

80
00:05:34,333 --> 00:05:39,299
The reflog is Git's local record of where
a ref has been, and the remote-tracking

81
00:05:39,339 --> 00:05:42,574
branch is your clone's record
of the server's branch.

82
00:05:42,614 --> 00:05:46,034
pull.ff only is stricter: the
pull stops and you decide.

83
00:05:46,074 --> 00:05:51,163
And an agreement: whoever rewrites a shared
branch announces it first, with the old

84
00:05:51,203 --> 00:05:53,326
tip and the command teammates need.

85
00:05:53,666 --> 00:05:59,155
Severity. SEV 3: a pull request is
unreviewable, and there's no wrong content on a

86
00:05:59,195 --> 00:06:00,301
protected branch.

87
00:06:02,141 --> 00:06:06,197
A picture helps. Think of two
editions of the same three chapters.

88
00:06:06,237 --> 00:06:10,542
Asha reprinted the chapters on new
paper: same text, new page numbers.

89
00:06:10,582 --> 00:06:15,693
That's the rebase. You still had the old
printing on your desk with two chapters of

90
00:06:15,733 --> 00:06:20,161
your own stapled behind it. Then you
bound both printings into one book.

91
00:06:20,201 --> 00:06:25,312
The book now contains chapters one to three
twice, and a reader who counts chapters

92
00:06:25,352 --> 00:06:29,595
is confused, while a reader who reads
the text finds nothing changed.

93
00:06:29,935 --> 00:06:34,658
The repair isn't to throw away the new
printing, which would undo Asha's work, and

94
00:06:34,698 --> 00:06:39,479
not to publish the double book. It is to
take your two chapters out and staple them

95
00:06:39,519 --> 00:06:40,874
behind the new printing.

96
00:06:41,214 --> 00:06:45,461
Where the picture breaks: with paper
you would know which pages are yours.

97
00:06:45,501 --> 00:06:50,329
In Git you have to find the boundary, and
it has a precise name: the old shared tip.

98
00:06:50,369 --> 00:06:55,080
Everything after it on your side is yours.
That boundary is what git rebase --onto

99
00:06:55,120 --> 00:06:57,977
<new-tip> <old-shared-tip>
takes as its arguments.

100
00:06:58,317 --> 00:07:03,351
And a rule for the whole standard: every
commit that matters gets identified by name

101
00:07:03,391 --> 00:07:08,364
in step 4, before anything moves. If you
can't write down three IDs, the old shared

102
00:07:08,404 --> 00:07:13,136
tip, the rebased tip and your tip before
the pull, you aren't ready for step 7.

103
00:07:14,976 --> 00:07:19,636
The eleven steps once more, in their
phases. The heavy line sits under step 5:

104
00:07:19,676 --> 00:07:24,697
nothing has changed up to there. The evidence
each step produces is filled in during

105
00:07:24,737 --> 00:07:25,240
the demo.

106
00:07:27,080 --> 00:07:32,011
From here on the screen shows the solution.
Is your own command log beside you?

107
00:07:34,351 --> 00:07:35,663
Then compare as we go.

108
00:07:36,003 --> 00:07:38,145
Step 1: inspect the state.

109
00:07:38,485 --> 00:07:43,564
git status -sb says the branch equals its
remote-tracking branch: locally nothing

110
00:07:43,604 --> 00:07:48,809
looks wrong. The graph shows two columns
that carry the same three subjects, joined

111
00:07:48,849 --> 00:07:50,199
by a merge at the top.

112
00:07:50,539 --> 00:07:54,434
What the pull request lists: nine
commits, three subjects twice.

113
00:07:54,474 --> 00:07:56,771
What it shows as changed: three files.

114
00:07:57,111 --> 00:07:58,948
Step 2: inspect refs.

115
00:07:59,288 --> 00:08:03,848
The local branch, the remote-tracking
branch and the server agree on 6e7ebbd.

116
00:08:03,888 --> 00:08:08,687
So this isn't a stale view or a display
problem on the platform: the nine commits

117
00:08:08,727 --> 00:08:10,658
are what the repository contains.

118
00:08:10,998 --> 00:08:16,089
Step 3: inspect the reflog. Two reflogs,
read together from the bottom up.

119
00:08:16,129 --> 00:08:20,110
Predict what kind of entry
you'll find at the top of each.

120
00:08:20,150 --> 00:08:21,219
Say it out loud.

121
00:08:26,059 --> 00:08:31,147
Your branch: a fast-forward to the shared
tip, two commits of your own, then "pull:

122
00:08:31,187 --> 00:08:35,409
Merge made by the 'ort' strategy".
The remote-tracking branch: "pull:

123
00:08:35,449 --> 00:08:40,352
forced-update". The server's branch was
replaced, and your pull, configured with

124
00:08:40,392 --> 00:08:44,058
pull.rebase=false, merged the
replacement with what you had.

125
00:08:44,398 --> 00:08:49,373
Step 4: identify the old branch
state. Three commits, by name.

126
00:08:49,413 --> 00:08:54,873
The shared tip before the rebase:
4967e71. The rebased tip: d4fd03c.

127
00:08:54,913 --> 00:08:57,623
Your tip before the pull: 57b5972.

128
00:08:57,963 --> 00:09:00,815
The merge commit confirms
two of them as its parents.

129
00:09:01,155 --> 00:09:06,213
Step 5: understand what changed. Are
the two copies the same changes?

130
00:09:06,553 --> 00:09:10,194
Three lines with an equals sign:
the same changes under new IDs.

131
00:09:10,234 --> 00:09:14,910
The dates agree with that: a rebase keeps
the author date and sets a new committer

132
00:09:14,950 --> 00:09:17,038
date. Nothing was lost by the rebase.

133
00:09:17,378 --> 00:09:22,312
Here is the heavy line under step 5.
Before we cross it, try it now, on paper.

134
00:09:22,352 --> 00:09:27,223
Thirty seconds. Write down the three IDs
from memory: the old shared tip, the

135
00:09:27,263 --> 00:09:29,901
rebased tip, and your tip before the pull.

136
00:09:32,241 --> 00:09:37,750
4967e71, d4fd03c and 57b5972. Up to here
nothing has changed in any repository

137
00:09:37,790 --> 00:09:43,512
except remote-tracking refs. You know what
happened, by whom, in which order, and

138
00:09:43,552 --> 00:09:44,864
you have three IDs.

139
00:09:45,204 --> 00:09:47,864
Step 6: preserve. 🟢 SAFE.

140
00:09:48,204 --> 00:09:52,782
Two anchors. One makes the current,
merged state restorable and comparable.

141
00:09:52,822 --> 00:09:57,091
The other holds the only commits that
exist in one clone alone: yours.

142
00:09:57,431 --> 00:10:00,705
Step 7 is the options table
from the concept section.

143
00:10:00,745 --> 00:10:05,081
Chosen: keep the rebased series,
replay only your two commits onto it.

144
00:10:05,421 --> 00:10:11,136
Step 8: restore the correct history. 🟡
CAUTION: git reset --keep moves the local

145
00:10:11,176 --> 00:10:17,034
branch and refuses to overwrite local
changes. And git rebase --onto replaces your

146
00:10:17,074 --> 00:10:18,904
two commits with new ones.

147
00:10:19,244 --> 00:10:23,996
Quick quiz. After these two commands,
what shape does the branch have?

148
00:10:24,036 --> 00:10:28,513
A, two columns joined by a merge,
as before. B, one straight line.

149
00:10:28,553 --> 00:10:29,335
Your answer?

150
00:10:40,675 --> 00:10:46,580
B. A straight line: Asha's three rebased
commits and your two on top, 021ec35 and

151
00:10:46,620 --> 00:10:47,167
5e4c03f.

152
00:10:47,507 --> 00:10:49,208
Before publishing, two proofs.

153
00:10:49,548 --> 00:10:53,005
The range-diff pairs both of your
commits with an equals sign.

154
00:10:53,045 --> 00:10:57,348
And the diff between the preserved merged
state and the rebuilt branch prints

155
00:10:57,388 --> 00:11:00,224
nothing. The repair changed
history and no content.

156
00:11:00,564 --> 00:11:04,712
Step 9: update the pull request
safely. 🔴 DANGEROUS: a forced push.

157
00:11:04,752 --> 00:11:09,712
The five answers. What it changes: the
server's branch, to a commit that doesn't

158
00:11:09,752 --> 00:11:14,899
descend from the old one. What it can
destroy: commits a teammate pushed after your

159
00:11:14,939 --> 00:11:19,587
fetch. Preview: git fetch, then compare
the server's value with the one you

160
00:11:19,627 --> 00:11:24,399
examined. Recovery: another clone's copy,
or the old ID from the push output.

161
00:11:24,439 --> 00:11:29,024
When appropriate: here, with an explicit
expected value, after telling the

162
00:11:29,064 --> 00:11:29,649
teammates.

163
00:11:29,989 --> 00:11:33,802
The lease names 6e7ebbd, the
value you examined in step 2.

164
00:11:33,842 --> 00:11:37,920
The pull request view now: five
commits, the same three files.

165
00:11:38,260 --> 00:11:42,531
The teammate. Asha's clone stood at the
rebased tip, of which the new tip is a

166
00:11:42,571 --> 00:11:43,139
descendant.

167
00:11:43,479 --> 00:11:47,393
For her, git pull --ff-only
is the whole repair.

168
00:11:47,733 --> 00:11:52,629
Step 10: explain. Three sentences, no
blame, in the textbook's wording: "Asha

169
00:11:52,669 --> 00:11:57,694
rebased the shared branch onto main while
I had two unpushed commits on the old

170
00:11:57,734 --> 00:12:02,631
version. My git pull merged the old and
the rebased commits, and I pushed the

171
00:12:02,671 --> 00:12:08,017
result, so the pull request listed both. I
rebuilt the branch as the rebased commits

172
00:12:08,057 --> 00:12:12,312
plus mine; no content changed, and
reviews of the diff stay valid."

173
00:12:12,652 --> 00:12:14,496
Step 11: prevent.

174
00:12:17,836 --> 00:12:22,515
The setting, the cleanup of the anchors
after verification, and the check: each

175
00:12:22,555 --> 00:12:27,115
subject exactly once, five commits, no
merge commit, both clones equal to the

176
00:12:27,155 --> 00:12:27,534
server.

177
00:12:27,874 --> 00:12:32,638
And there are all eleven steps on one sheet,
each with its command and the evidence

178
00:12:32,678 --> 00:12:35,474
it produced. The first
five rows changed nothing.

179
00:12:35,814 --> 00:12:36,908
The lease refuses.

180
00:12:37,248 --> 00:12:41,507
"Stale info". The server's branch is
no longer at the ID you examined.

181
00:12:41,547 --> 00:12:45,621
The lease did its job: it turned a
silent overwrite into a refusal.

182
00:12:45,961 --> 00:12:51,463
The tempting move. The lease is "in the
way". 🔴 DANGEROUS: a bare git push --force

183
00:12:51,503 --> 00:12:53,896
sets the server's ref with no check.

184
00:12:54,236 --> 00:12:58,070
The push succeeds. Count the commits
on the server's branch: five.

185
00:12:58,110 --> 00:13:00,124
Asha's new commit isn't among them.

186
00:13:00,464 --> 00:13:01,838
The recovery, in her clone.

187
00:13:02,178 --> 00:13:07,020
Her fetch reports a forced update. git
cherry lists what her branch has that the

188
00:13:07,060 --> 00:13:12,146
server's lacks. The line that matters is
the last one with a plus sign: cfee272, her

189
00:13:12,186 --> 00:13:17,210
commit. She anchors it, moves her branch
to the server's with --keep, picks the one

190
00:13:17,250 --> 00:13:21,604
commit, and pushes: an ordinary
fast-forward. Six commits on the server.

191
00:13:21,944 --> 00:13:26,221
The lesson: when a lease refuses, the
correct reaction is step 2 again.

192
00:13:26,261 --> 00:13:29,505
Fetch, look at what arrived,
and rebuild on top of it.

193
00:13:29,545 --> 00:13:31,207
The refusal was information.

194
00:13:33,047 --> 00:13:34,695
Five mistakes to watch for.

195
00:13:35,035 --> 00:13:39,435
Calling it a display problem on GitHub.
Root cause: the commit list and the file

196
00:13:39,475 --> 00:13:43,931
view answer different questions; git
ls-remote and a local log show that the nine

197
00:13:43,971 --> 00:13:44,874
commits are real.

198
00:13:45,214 --> 00:13:49,551
Blaming the lease or the rebase. Root
cause: the lease protects the server's

199
00:13:49,591 --> 00:13:54,217
commits, not unpublished commits in a
teammate's clone, and the rebase changed no

200
00:13:54,257 --> 00:13:56,866
content; the merge pull
joined the two series.

201
00:13:57,206 --> 00:14:02,145
Forcing the old series back. Root cause:
it undoes the teammate's rebase and makes

202
00:14:02,185 --> 00:14:05,546
her clone diverge, when only
two commits needed to move.

203
00:14:05,886 --> 00:14:09,905
Rebasing onto the new tip without
--onto and the old shared tip.

204
00:14:09,945 --> 00:14:15,232
Root cause: without the boundary, Git treats
the old copies of the shared commits as

205
00:14:15,272 --> 00:14:16,944
yours and replays them too.

206
00:14:17,284 --> 00:14:22,335
Replacing a refused lease with --force.
Root cause: the refusal means the server's

207
00:14:22,375 --> 00:14:27,427
branch moved after you examined it, and a
bare force overwrites whatever moved it.

208
00:14:29,267 --> 00:14:34,057
Now, out of the lab. Two engineers share
a branch for an online feature store.

209
00:14:34,097 --> 00:14:38,392
One rebases it onto main on Wednesday
evening and pushes with a lease.

210
00:14:38,432 --> 00:14:42,356
The other starts Thursday with
git pull and git push, as always.

211
00:14:42,396 --> 00:14:46,196
At ten, a reviewer asks why the
pull request has nine commits.

212
00:14:46,536 --> 00:14:51,273
The second engineer leads the repair, by
the eleven steps, and posts in the pull

213
00:14:51,313 --> 00:14:56,170
request before the forced push: "This branch
will be force-pushed once in the next

